<section id="basis">
<h2>4 Basis and Criteria</h2>
<p>The arithmetic basis and comparison criteria are defined in this section. The public guide supplies teaching inputs; the external references supply the physical relation and decimal unit definitions.</p>
<h3>4.1 Theory and equations</h3>
<p>Compatible multiplicative units are converted by equating the same physical quantity in source and target units. With magnitude <var>x</var> and scale <var>c</var> relative to a common unit, the numerical conversion is <var>x</var><sub>target</sub> = <var>x</var><sub>source</sub> <var>c</var><sub>source</sub> / <var>c</var><sub>target</sub>. This is the factor method described in <a href="#ref-conversion">Reference 1, Appendix B.3</a>; offset temperature conversions are outside this equation.</p>
<div class="formula">1 kPa = 1000 Pa; 1 kN = 1000 N; 1 mm = 0.001 m.</div>
<p>The decimal prefixes are defined in <a href="#ref-prefix">Reference 2, Section 4.3, Table 5</a>. Pressure is normal force per area, so the force on a uniformly loaded planar surface is expressed below.</p>
<div class="formula"><var>F</var> = <var>p</var> <var>A</var>; Pa × m² = N.</div>
<p><var>F</var> is normal surface-force magnitude, <var>p</var> is uniform pressure and <var>A</var> is loaded area. The relation is rearranged from <a href="#ref-physics">Reference 3, Section 14.1, Equation 14.3</a>; the pascal is expressed as newtons per square metre in <a href="#ref-prefix">Reference 2, Section 4.2.1, Table 3</a>.</p>
<h3>4.2 Physical and numerical criteria</h3>
<p>Uniform pressure over the stated area is an idealization marked ASSUMED - to be confirmed for any physical application. The example inputs are used as specified teaching magnitudes, without assigning measurement uncertainty or material properties.</p>
<p>The report's arithmetic criterion is agreement with the hand-calculated anchors to a relative tolerance of 10<sup>−12</sup>. This is a numerical regression criterion selected in the report tests, identified in <a href="#repository-records">Section 9.2</a>; it is not a material, load or operating limit. Displayed inputs are also multiplied to check the displayed headline result.</p>
<p>Capacity remains NOT_EVALUATED until the allowable load and its governing criterion are supplied. Net force remains NOT_EVALUATED until the pressure on the opposing face is supplied. These dispositions are recorded in <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="method">
<h2>5 Methodology and Interfaces</h2>
<p>The quantity interface is applied from explicitly labelled inputs to a force output and calculation record. Pint {{ data.versions.pint }} and NumPy {{ data.versions.numpy }} are the installed calculation dependencies represented by this copy.</p>
<h3>5.1 Inputs and execution</h3>
<p>A numeric scalar or array, a unit expression and an optional source label are accepted by <code>TrackedQuantity(value, unit, source)</code>. The pressure, area and thickness magnitudes below are read from the public guide rather than duplicated as report constants.</p>
<pre><code>pressure = TrackedQuantity(pressure_value, "kPa", source="Public guide")
area = TrackedQuantity(area_value, "m**2", source="Public guide")
pressure.check_dimensions("Pa")
force = (pressure.to("Pa") * area).to("kN")
audit = CalculationAuditLog()
audit.add_input("pressure", pressure)
audit.add_input("area", area)
audit.add_step("Convert pressure, multiply by area, convert force.")
audit.add_output("force", force)
record = audit.to_dict()</code></pre>
<p>The conversion returns a new quantity with its prior history and a conversion entry. Multiplication combines the operand histories and adds the arithmetic operation; the original inputs remain available. The dimensional check establishes compatibility with a pressure unit, without validating its physical origin.</p>
<h3>5.2 Outputs</h3>
<p>The outputs are a numeric magnitude, its canonical unit and an operation history. Dictionary serialization includes source labels, operation labels and timestamps; the audit interface groups named inputs, outputs and descriptive steps.</p>
<p>In this report's embedded calculation record, volatile timestamps are omitted while the source, operation and unit fields are preserved. The audit description is supplied by the caller; it is not an executed-equation certificate. The source files and their content digests are listed in <a href="#repository-records">Section 9.2</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="worked-example">
<h2>6 Worked example</h2>
<p>The guide's explicit pressure and area are multiplied through the public interface. An explicit conversion is applied to make the force unit unambiguous.</p>
<p>The inputs are <span data-value="pressure_kpa">{{ display.pressure_kpa }}</span> kPa and <span data-value="area_m2">{{ display.area_m2 }}</span> m². The converted pressure is <span data-value="pressure_pa">{{ display.pressure_pa }}</span> Pa. The resulting surface force is <span data-value="force_n">{{ display.force_n }}</span> N, expressed as <span data-value="force_kn">{{ display.force_kn }}</span> kN.</p>
<p>A separate length conversion from the same guide converts <span data-value="thickness_mm">{{ display.thickness_mm }}</span> mm to <span data-value="thickness_m">{{ display.thickness_m }}</span> m. That thickness is not used as a structural property of the loaded surface.</p>
<p>The public teaching inputs and their arithmetic locations are identified in <a href="#repository-records">the repository record</a>. The physical assumptions and unavailable capacity inputs are recorded in <a href="#appendix-a">the data register</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="limits">
<h2>7 Limits of Use</h2>
<p>The result boundaries are stated here; the associated evidence and handling actions are given in <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a>. Dimensional compatibility alone does not establish load origin, sign convention, pressure datum, material resistance or uncertainty.</p>
<p>Raw numeric arguments at decorated interfaces remain raw; unknown inferred fields and unknown policy quantity types can pass through. The strict policy flag rejects a mismatched unit only when automatic conversion is also disabled. Required inputs therefore need explicit type, unit and physical checks at the calling boundary.</p>
<p>Custom commodity definitions encode energy equivalents without a published basis in the inspected definition file. Their absolute factors are not evaluated here. The environmental length alias <code>nm</code> denotes a nautical mile in its adapter but a nanometre in the main registry; unambiguous unit names are required.</p>
<p>Provenance records are caller-labelled and mutable through serialization. The lineage rendering connects recorded inputs and outputs without reconstructing exact dependency paths. These records are calculation aids, without tamper evidence or proof of measurement origin.</p>
</section>
<section id="recommendations">
<h2>8 Recommendations</h2>
<p>The actions below are tied to the physical and interface limitations identified in this report. No structural acceptance is assigned to the example force.</p>
<ul><li>Use explicit quantities and target units at calculation boundaries.
<ul><li>Reject missing or untracked required inputs, and distinguish force from pressure before arithmetic, using the dimensional relation in <a href="#basis">Section 4</a> and the public interface in <a href="#method">Section 5</a>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Supply the physical acceptance basis before applying the surface force to a component.
<ul><li>Define the pressure datum, opposing pressure, loaded geometry, allowable load and governing criterion. Confirm the uniform-pressure idealization against the intended loading, as required by the relation in <a href="#ref-physics">Reference 3</a>.</li></ul></li>
<li>Bound the additional interfaces before wider use.
<ul><li>Use full length-unit names in shared data, document commodity-factor sources before absolute energy comparisons, and specify both policy switches when rejection is required. These actions address the inspected behaviours listed in <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a>.</li></ul></li>
</ul>
</section>
